<v Stefan>die Worte, die uns sozusagen dann Anlass geben, jetzt wieder Fundamente zu bauen.

02:34:27.362 --> 02:34:30.042
<v Clips>Ich denke, dass die Leute in den Tension zwischen den beiden Dingen leben.

02:34:30.122 --> 02:34:32.962
<v Clips>Und ich denke, was wir sehen, ist die collapse von all rivalen Systems.

02:34:33.482 --> 02:34:36.182
<v Clips>Eine meiner Lieblings-Fähigkeiten von Psychologie ist von einem Mann.

02:34:36.862 --> 02:34:38.902
<v Clips>John Bowlby, der hat sich an den Attachment-Researchen.

02:34:39.082 --> 02:34:42.782
<v Clips>Er sagt, all of life ist eine seriese von daringen Explorations von einem Secure-Base.

02:34:43.182 --> 02:34:47.062
<v Clips>Wir brauchen das Secure-Base. Und das Secure-Base, das ist über deine Eltern,

02:34:47.282 --> 02:34:48.742
<v Clips>die deine Eltern, die haben, die sich an der Attachment.

02:34:49.162 --> 02:34:54.482
<v Clips>Aber es ist auch über eine Secure-Homentown, eine Safe-Neinbarung.

02:34:55.022 --> 02:34:59.542
<v Clips>Aber es ist auch über eine Moral-Order. The sense that you live within a coherent

02:34:59.542 --> 02:35:02.482
<v Clips>moral order and you can make the decisions of your life based on this.

02:35:02.782 --> 02:35:06.942
<v Clips>There was a historian named George Marsden who wrote once that what gave Martin

02:35:06.942 --> 02:35:10.962
<v Clips>Luther King's rhetoric such power was his sense that there was a moral order

02:35:10.962 --> 02:35:12.482
<v Clips>woven into the fabric of universe.

02:35:12.962 --> 02:35:16.502
<v Clips>That segregation was not just wrong sometimes. Segregation was always wrong.

02:35:16.722 --> 02:35:19.202
<v Clips>Slavery is always wrong under all circumstances.

02:35:19.822 --> 02:35:23.342
<v Clips>And so that gives you a sense of security if you have a sense that,

02:35:23.462 --> 02:35:25.302
<v Clips>yes, that right and wrong are permanent.

02:35:25.742 --> 02:35:29.522
<v Clips>And we took that away. We privatized morality. We told people,

02:35:29.702 --> 02:35:31.242
<v Clips>it's up to you to come up with your own values.

02:35:31.682 --> 02:35:34.202
<v Clips>And if your name is Aristotle or Nietzsche, maybe you can do this.

02:35:34.322 --> 02:35:36.062
<v Clips>Most of us can't come up with our own values.

02:35:36.262 --> 02:35:40.762
<v Clips>And we're left in sort of a formless world that gives no security.

02:35:41.202 --> 02:35:45.922
<v Clips>Way back in 1955, a great columnist named Walter Lippmann wrote a book in which

02:35:45.922 --> 02:35:50.762
<v Clips>he wrote that if what is right and wrong is just what each individual invents

02:35:50.762 --> 02:35:54.502
<v Clips>based on his or her feelings, we have left the bounds of civilization.

02:35:54.502 --> 02:35:58.882
<v Clips>Und ich denke, wir haben diese geblieben. Es ist eine Loss der sozialen Sicherheit.

02:35:59.482 --> 02:36:03.482
<v Clips>Freunde und Familie sind verweakten. Die Gemeinschaftsbasis ist verweakten.

02:36:03.902 --> 02:36:05.382
<v Clips>Und die Moral-Base ist verweakten.

02:36:06.402 --> 02:36:09.682
<v Stefan>So, also die Kunden am Ende gehen verloren.

02:36:09.982 --> 02:36:14.262
